WEBVTT

00:00:00.231 --> 00:00:04.940
FedRAMP uses impact levels to help categorize cloud systems based on the potential impact

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.190
of a security incident.

00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:09.000
Common impact levels include Low, Moderate, and High.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.000
Impact levels help determine appropriate security requirements based on risk.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.170
A system that could create higher impact if compromised generally requires a stronger set

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:18.000
of safeguards,

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.000
controls, documentation, and monitoring.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.740
Low impact systems may involve information or functions where a security event would have

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:25.280
limited adverse effect.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:28.500
Moderate and High impact systems involve progressively greater concern for

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:29.425
confidentiality,

00:00:29.425 --> 00:00:30.980
integrity, or availability.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:35.110
These categories help agencies and cloud providers align security effort with system

00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:36.000
sensitivity.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.110
The goal is not to apply the same level of rigor to every cloud service,

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:41.980
but to match expectations to risk and mission context.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:47.000
Impact level affects control baselines, assessment expectations, monitoring activities,

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.000
and the evidence that stakeholders need to review.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:53.040
It also helps frame conversations about whether a service is appropriate for a specific

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:54.270
agency use case.

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:59.000
Understanding impact levels is useful for engineers and managers because design decisions

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.000
can affect how a cloud service supports the expected security posture.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.115
The practical message is that FedRAMP authorization is risk based.

00:01:05.115 --> 00:01:07.920
Higher impact does not simply mean more paperwork;

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:11.220
it means a stronger need to show that security controls are implemented and maintained

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:12.000
appropriately.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.970
Impact levels also influence planning.

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:17.090
Teams need to understand the intended customer, data, mission use,

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:21.530
and operating environment early enough to design controls and evidence processes that fit

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:22.840
the expected level.
